igh-quality polls (YouGov, NYT/Siena, Wedgewood) consistently show Masterson up three to five points", "Open seat in an R+8 state after two Kelly wins; the popular incumbent is term-limited and off the ballot", "Expert raters converge on Lean R (five of seven), and prediction markets price Holscher near 25%", "A Democratic-favorable national environment and debates over taxes, schools, and data-center incentives keep the race within reach"], "rationale": "Polling, expert ratings, and prediction markets all point the same direction: Masterson holds a mid-single-digit lead in a state that leans Republican, with the strongest recent surveys showing a three-to-five-point edge. The open seat and a favorable Democratic environment keep Holscher competitive, but nothing in the evidence justifies moving the race out of Lean Republican.", "watch": "A late Democratic surge riding the favorable national environment, or turnout effects from the concurrent Senate race and Trump's Wichita rally, could shift the margin in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Laura Kelly (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Kansas is holding an open governor's race now that two-term Democrat Laura Kelly is term-limited. State Senator Cindy Holscher, a Democrat, faces Senate President Ty Masterson, a Republican, in a state that has leaned about eight points Republican at the presidential level even as Kelly won twice. Recent polls from YouGov, the New York Times/Siena, and Wedgewood all show Masterson ahead by three to five points, and earlier surveys pointed the same way aside from some Democratic-sponsored ones. Most expert raters call the race Lean Republican, a couple see it slightly closer, and prediction markets give Holscher roughly a one-in-four chance.\n\nThe campaign has centered on taxes, education funding, and a data-center incentive law that has stirred debate. A Democratic-friendly national environment and Kelly's past wins keep the contest competitiv